The fixes for CVE-2026-135804 (GHSA-cpww-5vpx-72x7) bound the regex work of a JsonCrawler::find() call, and evaluate filters and absolute queries once per call. Chained recursive descent isn't bounded. Each ..* segment takes the descendants of every node selected so far. A node nested in other selected nodes is counted once per such ancestor. So the size of the node list is a power of the depth, one power per segment. RFC 9535 allows these duplicates.

$document = 'null';
for ($i = 0; $i < 40; ++$i) {
    $document = '{"a":'.$document.',"b":1}';
}

// 484 bytes
(new JsonCrawler($document))->fi

The sandbox checks that a template may call toString() on an object before converting it to a string. Since Twig 3.23.0, assignments like x = obj, and the destructuring forms [a] = list and {a} = obj, evaluate to the value that they assign. The sandbox didn't check that value, so a sandboxed template could call a denied toString() method:

{{ (x = obj) }}
{{ ({secret: s} = obj)|join }}

No tag needs to be allowed.

The sandbox now checks the result of assignments like the result of filters and functions. {% do x = obj %} and {% if (x = obj) %} still work, since they don't convert the value to

The join filter converted a value that isn't iterable with (array) $value. For an object, that gives every property, including the private and protected ones. A sandboxed template allowed to use join could therefore print properties that the policy denies, and that templates can't read otherwise:

{{ user|join(',') }}

A value that isn't iterable is now joined as a single item. A Stringable object prints its checked string, and other objects throw, as with the first and last filters.

The escaper considered x.attr, x['key'] and x.method() safe whenever {% import ... as x %} appeared anywhere in the template. Macro calls haven't needed this rule since Twig 3.0. It only matched ordinary variables that have the name of an alias imported in another block or macro, and printed them without escaping:

{% block header %}{% import _self as item %}{{ item.hello() }}{% endblock %}
{% block content %}{% for item in items %}{{ item.body }}{% endfor %}{% endblock %}

Here, item.body is printed raw. This leads to XSS when the value comes from an attacker. The same happens with an import

The guard tag compiles only one of its branches, depending on whether a callable exists. The other branch was still parsed, though. The extends, use, macro and block tags in it registered their effects on the template. The sandbox doesn't see these tags, since that branch isn't compiled. A sandboxed template allowed to use guard could therefore extend a template, import blocks and define macros, while the policy doesn't allow these tags:

{% guard filter upper %}{% else %}{% extends "parent.html" %}{% endguard %}

